A||||5||prof bobo||restoration/modification Q's||||1||
Z||000000||prof bobo||11-02-2000||11:36 AM||||I just about got my 85GT stripped to the bone, ready to start patching rust and repainting.  A couple of questions, though.<P>First, is there a good reason why the wire harnesses are routed along the outside of the frame when there are plenty of internal frame channels into which they could have been placed?  My guess is that it was done for ease of manufacture -quicker (cheaper) to tack it on than fish it through.  I'm going to unwrap, inspect/repair and encase it in heat-shrink tubing and, unless someone can point out some dumb obvious reason why I shouldn't, I plan on running it through existing cavities in the frame.<P>Second, I see quite a few spots on the floor of the frame, holes purposely stamped out into which galvanized metal plates have been inserted and sealed with some kind of what looks like rubber gasket or sealing compound.<BR>I got rust aroung a couple of these and I'm probably going to knock them all out and weld  some new sheet metal over these holes for a permanent repair.  Good idea or bad idea?  Anyone even know why it's made that way?<P>That's it for now, back to grindin' rust . . .||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||11-02-2000||06:01 PM||||Best reason for the wire harness being on the outside - - - - wire chafing would be a problem inside channels. Inspect it and rewrap, install in original position.<BR>No reason why you can't wild the bottom of the floor pan up that I can think of. Who knows, with good door seals it may float!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tigger||11-03-2000||12:23 AM||||I agree, weld in the drain plugs and run the wires along the factory routings.  I've swapped the entire electrical system out of my Fiero and I see no advantage of wiring it any other way.<P>Yea, you might be able to run the visible wires under the hood and deck so they are out of the way and less noticable but that's all I'd do.  As far as the interior harness you can't see it anyway.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue.gif"> ||||reg||4
Z||000003||prof bobo||11-03-2000||10:44 AM||||I was only considering the 'outside' harnesses anyway, get 'em out of dirt's way. I've been considering filling the frame cavities with expanding foam when I'm all done to keep anything from ever getting back in there.<P>As far as the chafing issue goes, why would it be more likely to get chafed inside of the frame rails vs sandwiched between the frame and body skin?  Besides, my plan is to shrinkwrap, cover with nylon spiral wrap and then another layer of shrink wrap.  It would take a _lot_ of chafing to get through that, eh?||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||11-03-2000||12:22 PM||||I don't see the problem either, unless you don't plan to use grommets on the openings where you feed the wires.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tigger||11-03-2000||01:02 PM||||Go ahead with the electrical routing, but I wouldn't recommend filling the subframe cavities with foam.  It will trap moisture in there from changes in temperature and dew and rust the body from the inside out.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 11-03-2000).]||||reg||1
